# `sink` on an affine type. Accepted and then SILENTLY IGNORED until 2026-08-14:
# measured with a counting C shim, a `sink File` callee left the handle live and
# unclosed at return -- it borrowed exactly like a default parameter, so the
# declared mode did nothing at all.
#
# Refused rather than implemented. Passing an affine value already BORROWS
# (docs/spec/14-ffi.md, §25), which is what a caller wants; a consuming callee
# would have to free at ITS scope exit while the destructor is emitted at the
# owner's, which is the double free of FRICTION #43. `inout` is refused for the
# same reason -- there is no copy-back for a value with one owner.
# expect: which an affine type cannot be
# expect: Pass it plainly

handle R:
    free: hc
extern fn ho(i: int) -> R
extern fn hc(h: R) -> int
extern fn hu(h: R) -> int

fn take(h: sink R) -> int:
    return hu(h)

fn main():
    println("unreachable")
